before:
```typescript
function failureText(failure: unknown): string {
  try {
    throw failure;
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof Error) return error.message;
    return "Not an Error object";
  }
}
console.log(failureText(new Error("Door closed")));
console.log(failureText("Door closed"));
```
This prints Door closed, then Not an Error object. failureText takes any supplied failure. In this isolated simulation, throw sends it straight to catch(error); a real application would catch a failing operation instead. instanceof Error checks whether the caught value is an Error instance. Only that branch reads .message. new Error("Door closed") constructs an Error with that message. console.log displays the returned text.

